<!--
  AmOS System UI — Android **Activity** fragment (real-device step).

  This is NOT a complete manifest, and unlike the permissions/components fragments
  it is not `<manifest>`-level: these entries belong **inside** the `<activity>`
  element Tauri's template writes. When the Tauri-Android project is generated
  (cargo tauri android init), merge them into that `<activity>` block in
  gen/android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml — as sibling `<intent-filter>`
  elements of the template's own MAIN/LAUNCHER filter. The template block, verbatim
  from `strings ~/.cargo/bin/cargo-tauri`, is:

      <activity android:name=".MainActivity" …>
          <intent-filter>
              <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
              <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
              <!-- AndroidTV support -->
              <category android:name="android.intent.category.LEANBACK_LAUNCHER" />
          </intent-filter>
      </activity>

  WHY this file exists (REQ-A774 / F-AND-006): the filters below were hand-added
  during bring-up straight into the git-ignored generated manifest, so a clean
  checkout built an APK without them. Two product surfaces depend on them:

   * HOME — AmOS is an *optional* launcher. Without the filter the app cannot be
     picked as the device's default home app, so the system Home gesture can never
     return to AmOS and the whole launcher/dock surface is unreachable on device.
   * DIAL — AmOS is a *candidate* default phone app. Without the filter it never
     appears in the "Default phone app" list, so `ROLE_DIALER` (and with it
     `AmosInCallService`, see AndroidManifest.components.xml) can never be granted,
     and the dialer falls back to the `ACTION_CALL` path only. This surface needs
     **two** filters, not one — see the note on the pair below (REQ-A1873 /
     F-AND-018): a `tel`-scoped filter alone does not qualify the app at all.

  Neither absence throws: the feature simply never becomes available, which is why
  no compile and no permission check could see it. `scripts/android-glue-mirror.sh`
  now checks the generated manifest in both directions, so an entry that lives only
  in the generated file fails the build (docs/REAL_DEVICE_SYSTEM_UI_AUDIT.md §5.1).

  `android.intent.category.DEFAULT` is repeated in both filters on purpose — it is
  what makes the filter match an implicit `startActivity`/`queryIntentActivities`,
  and it is the only reason this file lists a name twice.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!-- Optional HOME (launcher): lets the user pick AmOS as the device's default
         home app in Settings, so the system Home gesture returns to AmOS. -->
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.HOME" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
    </intent-filter>

    <!-- Dialer candidate (ROLE_DIALER): lets the user make AmOS the default phone
         app so real Telecom Calls bind our AmosInCallService and show in AmOS's own
         in-call / incoming-call UI (docs/telephony.md).

         TWO filters, and the FIRST one is what makes the app eligible at all.
         RoleManager qualifies a package for `android.app.role.DIALER` by building
         an intent with action DIAL and **no data URI** — its RequiredComponent
         carries `mDataScheme=null` — and asking the PackageManager who handles it.
         A filter that declares `<data android:scheme="tel"/>` cannot match a
         no-data intent (`IntentFilter.matchData` answers NO_MATCH_DATA), so a
         single `tel`-scoped filter leaves AmOS absent from that query. Measured on
         the S5 (Android 14): `adb shell cmd package query-activities -a
         android.intent.action.DIAL` returned only `com.google.android.dialer`,
         and `cmd role add-role-holder android.app.role.DIALER com.amos.ai` failed
         with "Package does not qualify for the role". The bare filter (no <data>)
         is what the role's own query matches; the `tel` filter beside it is what
         handles `tel:` URIs. Dropping either one silently removes a surface — the
         role, or tel-link dialing — with nothing failing to build. -->
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.DIAL" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
    </intent-filter>

    <!-- `tel:` URI handler (dial links from browsers / contacts). Scoped to the
         scheme on purpose; on its own it does NOT qualify for ROLE_DIALER. -->
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.DIAL" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <data android:scheme="tel" />
    </intent-filter>

</manifest>
